<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left subtalar and talonavicular joints (M19.172)</content>. Dr. Holmquist-Ellery confirms post-traumatic arthritis of the posterior subtalar and talonavicular joints from the old gunshot wound, with the ankle joint spared. Normal inflammatory markers, a healed scar and no bone changes of infection on CT make chronic osteomyelitis unlikely. Non-operative care first, as he wishes. [S]<list><item>A rigid ankle-foot orthosis molded by the orthotist, and a rocker-sole shoe.</item><item>Physical therapy weekly for six weeks, with a home exercise program.</item><item>Naproxen 500 mg twice daily with food for up to six weeks, since kidney function and home blood pressure are normal; continue diclofenac gel.</item><item>Subtalar injection if pain stays above 4 of 10 after four to six weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Retained metal fragments in the left heel (Z18.12)</content>. The fragments lie in soft tissue, not in a joint, bone or fluid-filled space, which is where lead is absorbed; his blood lead is low. Removing them would mean operating through scarred heel skin for no benefit. [S]<list><item>Leave the fragments in place.</item><item>Repeat blood lead only if symptoms of lead toxicity appear or a fragment moves into a joint.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Raised blood pressure reading at intake (R03.0)</content>. Home average 124/78 and clinic 128/80 mmHg: no hypertension. [S]<list><item>Blood pressure at visits; yearly screening.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain on walking (0 to 10 numeric rating scale)</td><td>Every visit and after each treatment trial</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7 of 10, unchanged on diclofenac gel</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Function: Foot and Ankle Ability Measure, activities of daily living subscale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>52% (baseline)</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Joint examination: hindfoot and ankle motion, alignment, swelling and gait</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Subtalar motion about half and painful; talonavicular tenderness; mild hindfoot varus; antalgic gait</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise, physical therapy and brace use</td><td>Every visit; physical therapy weekly for six weeks</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Brace casting today; physical therapy booked weekly for six weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID safety: blood pressure, creatinine and blood count; stomach symptoms</td><td>Before an oral NSAID, then every 6 to 12 months on long-term use</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Before naproxen: creatinine 0.96 mg/dL, eGFR 93, hemoglobin 14.6 g/dL at intake; home blood pressure average 124/78; no past stomach trouble</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging</td><td>At diagnosis or for surgical planning; not for routine follow-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weight-bearing X-rays at intake and CT today confirm the diagnosis and map the fragments</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood lead with retained bullet fragments</td><td>Once when fragments are found; repeat only if fragments lie in a joint or bone, or symptoms suggest lead toxicity</td><td>Completed today</td><td>2.4 ug/dL with four extra-articular fragments</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, with home readings to confirm or exclude hypertension</td><td>Every visit; home readings after a raised reading</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Clinic 128/80; home average 124/78 mmHg; no hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screening, sleep and coping</td><td>At baseline and periodically</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 6 at intake, from poor sleep with pain; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c in prediabetes</td><td>At diagnosis, then in three to four months and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>6.0% at intake; diabetes prevention program offered</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Food access and the food distribution program</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>The Community Health Representative is helping with the food distribution program application</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Traditional healing alongside clinic care, at his choice</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Offered again; he will decide in his own time; nothing further asked</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Orthopaedic foot and ankle consultation</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Holmquist-Ellery today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Walk a mile on uneven ground and ride with pain of 3 or less. In progress; target 1886-09-14 [S]</item><item>Decide about surgery with full information, in his own time. In progress; target 1886-09-14 [S]</item><item>Bring hemoglobin A1c below 5.7%. In progress; target 1886-10-26 [S]</item><item>Enough food through the month. In progress; target 1886-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026); Lead toxicity from retained bullet fragments: A systematic review and meta-analysis (2019); Indian Health Manual, Part 3, Chapter 30: Chronic Non-Cancer Pain Management (2026); Special General Memorandum 94-08: Statement of Policy for the Traditional Cultural Advocacy Program (1994); Hypertension in Adults: Screening (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Five-week review with the brace fitted and physical therapy under way. Call for redness or drainage at the old wound, or stomach pain or black stools on naproxen. Next scheduled visit 1886-08-10: five-week review: the brace, physical therapy, pain treatment and a subtalar injection. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
